<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albarracín</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albarracín is a small walled town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, Aragon, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on a rock above the Guadalaviar river. In the 11th century it was the seat of the Banu Razin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a>, and from about 1167–1170 it was an independent Christian lordship of the Azagra family until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> conquered it in 1284. Its medieval walls and houses make it one of Spain's best-preserved historic towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albarracin.jpg" alt="Albarracín" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albarrac%C3%ADn,_Teruel,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2014-01-10,_DD_120.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Albarracín lies in a meander of the Guadalaviar River, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> in Aragon, with the Sierra de Albarracín rising to the south and west. It is the capital of the mountainous comarca of that name. The town is surrounded by stony hills, red sandstone boulders and cliffs, which draw rock climbers, particularly boulderers. It stands near the ancient Roman city of Lobetum. In the Visigothic period it was called Santa María de Oriente, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> called the area Alcartam, probably from the earlier name Ercávida. Its present name is thought to come from the Banu Razin, so that it means the place of the sons of Razin; its official name until the 19th century was Ciudad de Santa María de Albarracín.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hawwara Berber dynasty of the Banu Razin made Albarracín its capital from the early 11th century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took it in 1104. From the later 12th century until 1300 it was an independent lordship, the Sinyoría d'Albarrazín, established after the partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Albarracín under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ruiz-de-azagra">Pedro Ruiz de Azagra</a>. Peter III of Aragon conquered it in 1284 and deposed the ruling House of Azagra. The last man to hold the title of Señor de Albarracín was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-nunez-i-de-lara">Juan Núñez I de Lara</a>; his son Juan Núñez II de Lara remained pretender until 1300, when the city and its lands were formally incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>.</p><p>A Jewish community lived in the town from before the 12th century, according to the earliest written records. It suffered antisemitic incidents until the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1492. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> control of the town shifted several times between Republican and Nationalist forces. A Republican offensive in July 1937 took it on 8 July, except the town hall and cathedral, where soldiers and civilians were besieged; Nationalist reinforcements retook it on 13 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Albarracín has been a National Monument since 1961 and received the Gold Medal for Merit in the Fine Arts in 1996. It has been proposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> for World Heritage status, and it has belonged to the association of the Most Beautiful Villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> since its founding. The 2007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 1,075 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albarracín” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lobetani are recorded mainly by Ptolemy in the 2nd century CE, who places them in the mountains of eastern Spain with a town called Lobeton, perhaps near <b>Albarracín</b> in modern Teruel.</blockquote><a href="/state/lobetani">Lobetani</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1170 the Navarrese noble Pedro Ruiz de Azagra obtained <b>Albarracín</b> from Ibn Mardanish of the Second Taifa of Murcia, whom he had served.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-albarracin">Lordship of Albarracín</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Albarracín</b> was a rugged frontier lordship of castles, pasture and timber between the taifas of Toledo, Zaragoza and Valencia.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-albarracin">Taifa of Albarracín</a></li><li><blockquote>Molina was a small border principality between the taifas of Toledo, Zaragoza and <b>Albarracín</b>, more a fortified lordship than a full kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-molina">Taifa of Molina</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 1092 Ibn Labbun had handed the town to the ruler of <b>Albarracín</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-murviedro">Taifa of Murviedro</a></li></ul></section>
